<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Italian Regency of Carnaro</h1><p class="st-meta">1919 CE – 1920 CE · capital Fiume</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Poet Gabriele D'Annunzio's self-proclaimed state in Fiume, a theatrical experiment that foreshadowed the style and rituals of Italian Fascism.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-yugoslavia">Kingdom of Yugoslavia</a> both claimed the port of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-state-of-fiume">Fiume</a>, today <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rijeka">Rijeka</a>, which had an Italian-speaking majority in the town. On 12 September 1919 the nationalist poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gabriele-dannunzio">Gabriele D'Annunzio</a> led some 2,600 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">legionaries</a> into Fiume and occupied it in defiance of the Italian government. In September 1920 he proclaimed the Italian Regency of Carnaro.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its constitution, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charter-of-carnaro">Charter of Carnaro</a>, drafted with the syndicalist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alceste-de-ambris">Alceste De Ambris</a>, organised citizens into corporations and declared music a fundamental institution of the state. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gabriele-dannunzio">D'Annunzio</a>'s balcony speeches, uniforms, salutes and mass rituals were later copied by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a>. The town lived on smuggling and piracy while blockaded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-rapallo-1920">Treaty of Rapallo</a> in November 1920 made <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-state-of-fiume">Fiume</a> a free state. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gabriele-dannunzio">D'Annunzio</a> refused to accept it, Italian troops and warships attacked in the '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bloody-christmas-1920">Bloody Christmas</a>' of December 1920, and he left in January 1921. The Free State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rijeka">Fiume</a> followed.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/italian-regency-of-carnaro.jpg" alt="Italian Regency of Carnaro" width="251" height="138"><figcaption>Image: Decius · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Free_State_of_Fiume_1920-1924.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1919 CE – 1920 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Fiume</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Gabriele D'Annunzio</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Italian, Croatian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/croatia">Croatia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Regency of Carnaro, D'Annunzio's Fiume, Reggenza Italiana del Carnaro</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1920&amp;focus=italian-regency-of-carnaro" aria-label="Italian Regency of Carnaro on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5023 578 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Italian Regency of Carnaro lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5343 834L5343 834M5343 834L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5343 834h0M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5343 834h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5343 834h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1920&amp;focus=italian-regency-of-carnaro">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
